From b1528ffe16d39078678b966325cee17de482e644 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 10:58:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] psxtests/psx13: Stat the file before making it read-only

FutimensTest() restores the file mode with chmod( "testfile1.tst",
fstat.st_mode ) after the read-only test case, but nothing in that
function ever fills fstat in.  The restoring chmod() therefore applies an
uninitialised mode.  UtimesTest() and UtimensatTest() do the stat() first;
FutimensTest() was missing it.

When the leftover stack contents happen to carry write permission the test
passes, which is what happens with GCC.  With clang the file stays
read-only and the following futimens( fd, NULL ) case fails with EACCES.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 testsuites/psxtests/psx13/test.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/testsuites/psxtests/psx13/test.c b/testsuites/psxtests/psx13/test.c
index 4dcd4143d6..632f525fe5 100644
--- a/testsuites/psxtests/psx13/test.c
+++ b/testsuites/psxtests/psx13/test.c
@@ -652,6 +652,14 @@ static void FutimensTest( void )
 
   /* Case: File is read-only and time's tv_nsec members are UTIME_NOW */
 
+  /*
+   * Remember the original mode so that it can be restored below.  Without
+   * this, fstat is read uninitialized and the restoring chmod() leaves the
+   * file without write permission, so the later successful test cases fail.
+   */
+  rv = stat( "testfile1.tst", &fstat );
+  rtems_test_assert( rv == 0 );
+
   /* Change file to be read-only */
   rv = chmod( "testfile1.tst", 06444 );
   rtems_test_assert( rv == 0 );
